{
  "requirement": "Repository validation MUST continue after a failed check and report every failed\ncheck it finds in one run, each as a {term}`requirements.ci.validation_error`.\nThis is a convention for every validation error; the requirement for each check\nstates which conditions that check reports. A file that cannot be parsed, such as\na malformed `habeas.toml`, `book.toml`, or evidence file, is one failed check:\nits error MAY report only the first parse or decoding problem in that file, and\nchecks that do not depend on its contents MUST still run.\n\nThe code that sequences the repository's checks carries out the first paragraph.\nA repository-validation function that collects validation errors MUST hold them\nin a parameter or local variable named `errors`, and a function with such a\nvariable is a site below; authoring code that collects errors only to abort is\nnot one. After one of its checks fails, a site MUST still run every later check\nthat does not depend on the failed check's result, including later checks in the\nsame loop iteration, and MUST NOT report one failure twice.\n\n::::{sites} error_accumulators\n```yaml\nlanguage: rust\npaths: [src, crates/habeas-sdk/src]\nexclude: [src/authoring/inputs.rs, src/authoring/tools.rs]\nrule:\n  kind: function_item\n  has:\n    stopBy: { kind: function_item }\n    any:\n      - kind: parameter\n        has: { field: pattern, regex: \"^errors$\" }\n      - kind: let_declaration\n        has: { field: pattern, regex: \"^errors$\" }\n  not:\n    inside:\n      stopBy: end\n      kind: mod_item\n      has: { field: name, regex: \"^tests$\" }\nmatch:\n  - \"fn check(value: &str, errors: &mut Vec<String>) {}\"\n  - |\n    fn run() {\n        let mut errors: BTreeSet<String> = BTreeSet::new();\n        check(\"\", &mut errors);\n    }\nno_match:\n  - \"fn check(value: &str, discovery_errors: &mut Vec<String>) {}\"\n  - |\n    fn run() {\n        let mut problems = Vec::new();\n        check(\"\", &mut problems);\n    }\n  - |\n    mod tests {\n        fn check(errors: &mut Vec<String>) {}\n    }\n```\n::::\n\n::::{violations} early_exits\n```yaml\nlanguage: rust\npaths: [src, crates/habeas-sdk/src]\nwithin: error_accumulators\nrule:\n  any:\n    - kind: try_expression\n    - kind: return_expression\n    - kind: continue_expression\n    - kind: break_expression\nmatch:\n  - |\n    fn check(v: &Value, errors: &mut Vec<String>) -> Option<u8> {\n        let n = v.as_u64()?;\n        if v.is_null() { errors.push(\"null\".into()); }\n        Some(n as u8)\n    }\n  - |\n    fn check(items: &[Value], errors: &mut Vec<String>) {\n        for item in items {\n            if !item.is_object() { errors.push(\"object\".into()); continue; }\n            if item.is_null() { errors.push(\"null\".into()); }\n        }\n    }\n  - |\n    fn check(v: &Value, errors: &mut Vec<String>) {\n        if !v.is_object() { errors.push(\"object\".into()); return; }\n        if v.is_null() { errors.push(\"null\".into()); }\n    }\n  - |\n    fn check(items: &[Value], errors: &mut Vec<String>) {\n        for item in items {\n            if !item.is_object() { errors.push(\"object\".into()); break; }\n        }\n        if items.is_empty() { errors.push(\"empty\".into()); }\n    }\nno_match:\n  - |\n    fn parse(v: &Value) -> Option<u8> {\n        let n = v.as_u64()?;\n        Some(n as u8)\n    }\n```\n::::",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations and where they are checked**\n\n1. *Validation continues after a failed check and reports every failure, each as a `validation_error`.* The sequencing code is `src/evidence.rs#fn[inspect_repository_with_progress]`. Three tests check it:\n   - `failed_checks_do_not_hide_later_independent_checks` feeds in many failures at once. They include invalid and reserved names, a missing `text`, several bad inputs in one record, and inline and misplaced `{fixme}` references. It asserts that each expected message appears, including several failures from the same loop iteration, such as `inputs[0]` reporting both `text[0]` and the unresolved definition.\n   - `configuration_and_dependency_failures_do_not_stop_later_checks` checks that every unresolvable term is reported. It also checks that a failing record does not stop later records and that coverage setup failures do not stop later checks.\n   - `collects_missing_orphan_format_false_and_changed_line_errors` and `schema_rejects_each_malformed_field_and_input_form` assert dozens of distinct findings from one run.\n\n   The messages asserted in these tests are prefixed with the file or source location and name the failed check, as the `requirements.ci.validation_error` definition describes. The format itself is not this requirement's concern.\n\n2. *A file that cannot be parsed is one failed check, and independent checks still run.* `configuration_and_dependency_failures_do_not_stop_later_checks` writes an invalid `habeas.toml` (`version = 2`), a malformed `book.toml` and a malformed evidence file (`ghost.json` containing `{`). It then asserts that the `src/main.rs` unknown-reference error and the missing-evidence error still appear. `status_loads_nearest_workspace_file_without_validating_authoring` checks the same for a malformed workspace file through the CLI. `inspection_progress_preserves_validation_and_finishes_each_phase` checks that an unparsable Rust source and invalid evidence JSON are both reported. `accepted_exceptions_cannot_waive_invalid_measurement_and_checks_still_collect_errors` checks a malformed `book.toml` together with an unparsable source.\n\n3. *MUST NOT report one failure twice.* Tests that check this:\n   - `failed_checks_do_not_hide_later_independent_checks` sorts and dedups the full error list.\n   - `configuration_and_dependency_failures_do_not_stop_later_checks` counts the `habeas.toml` and `book.toml` errors, and checks uniqueness for the coverage-configuration cases.\n   - `git_index_failure_is_reported_once_and_later_checks_still_run` counts the discovery error once.\n   - `an_unlistable_review_directory_is_reported_once` and `an_unreadable_record_is_reported_once` cover single-report cases.\n   - `failed_site_queries_do_not_repeat_errors_through_dependent_violations` covers a failing site query not being repeated through its dependent violation queries.\n   - `every_review_kind_loads_both_formats_and_reports_each_application_failure_once` and the coverage-check test assert uniqueness too.\n\n4. *The `errors` naming rule and the per-site behavior.* A site must hold its errors in a variable named `errors`, run later independent checks after a failure, and not report a failure twice. Each site upholds this on its own and has its own review, so I did not vet sites. The convention's code-level part, the sequencing function, is tested as shown above.\n\n**Site query `error_accumulators`**\n\nI ran it and scanned the matches. They include `access.rs` `fn[files]` and `fn[walk]` in the SDK, `citations.rs` `fn[validate]`, `fn[validate_review]` and `fn[parse_citation]`, and `evidence.rs` `fn[inspect_repository_with_progress]`, `fn[collect_definitions]`, `fn[files_with_extension]` and `fn[book_source_dir]`. All are genuine accumulators holding `errors`.\n\nI then searched `src` and `crates` for parameters or locals named `errors` and for accumulators named otherwise.\n- Test-module functions are correctly excluded by the `mod tests` clause.\n- `src/refactor/tests.rs` is a separate test file. Its only match is `let (errors, _)`, a tuple pattern the query does not match, so it is not wrongly selected.\n- Functions that name their collector differently, such as `load_definitions` and `reference_set_digest_for` (`failures`), say in comments that they abort on any failure, so they fall under the \"collects errors only to abort\" carve-out. `prepare_site` and `prepare_citation` in `src/authoring/inputs.rs`, and the helpers in `src/authoring/tools.rs`, are the excluded authoring files.\n- I found no `mut errors` parameter, which the `^errors$` pattern would miss.\n\nI found no place the query misses or wrongly selects.\n\n**Snippets**\n- The site query's `match` snippets cover the parameter form and the `let` form with a non-`Vec` type.\n- Its `no_match` snippets cover a differently named collector (`discovery_errors`), a `problems` local, and a function inside `mod tests`.\n- The violation query has one `match` snippet per construct (`?`, `continue`, `return`, `break`) and a `no_match` snippet for a non-site function using `?`.\n- The tests in `src/sites.rs` (`a_violation_query_needs_a_site_query`, `within_cannot_name_another_violation_query` and `failed_site_queries_do_not_repeat_errors_through_dependent_violations`) exercise the query and violation machinery.\n\n**Notes (not gaps)**\n- The violation query only flags early-exit constructs. It does not flag other ways of dropping a later check or reporting a failure twice, such as `unwrap` or `panic`. It is a heuristic.\n- The query ignores collectors held in struct fields, which the text's \"parameter or local variable\" rule also excludes.\n\nAll obligations that can be tested here are exercised with assertions, the site queries look accurate, and the snippets are adequate.",
